WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.440
In today's world it can feel like
there's no space for silence.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.760
Like we're always required to be
responding to something,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.200
to be saying something new.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.200
Whether that's answering
a text or an email,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.640
a tweet, a WhatsApp message
or even the phone.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.680
My name's Harriet Shawcross.
I'm a film-maker and a journalist

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.720
and I think we would all benefit

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.000
from a little bit more silence
in our lives.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.720
One of the reasons
I'm interested in silence

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.320
and in what can and can't be said

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.920
is that when I was a teenager

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.600
there was about a year when I didn't
really talk to people at school.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.360
I would answer direct questions
if a teacher asked them

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.520
or I would read out loud,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.400
but the kind of communication
that makes us human -

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.640
spontaneous conversation,
sharing jokes

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.360
was something that
I wasn't able to do.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.920
And it had a huge impact on me
and was really formative

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.320
in terms of how I relate to people
both professionally and personally.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.200
So what's so great about silence?

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.120
Well in the course
of researching my book

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.960
I came across several studies
into the impact

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.560
that silence can have
on both the body and the brain.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.440
In one study involving mice,

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.040
silence was shown to promote
the growth of brain cells

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.480
in the part of the brain responsible
for memory related to the senses.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.920
Scientists played mice
a selection of sounds

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.240
including baby mouse cries,
white noise, and silence

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.200
and observed that during silence

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.000
there was cell growth
in the hippocampus.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.240
Another study looked at the impact
of listening to music on the body

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.920
and the researchers found
that if silence was inserted

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.320
into a track of music

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.840
the blood pressure dropped, the heart
rate reduced and the subject relaxed

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.800
much more than when listening
to a relaxing piece of music.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.800
This only happened when the silence

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.880
was inserted in the middle
of the tune itself.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.960
It didn't work for silence at the
beginning or end of the experiment.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.040
So there's something about silence
that comes in the middle of noise

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.400
that's particularly beneficial.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.600
So silence can actually also
enable people to actually say things

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.280
that they've never been able
to say before.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.360
I looked into the formation
of the Samaritans,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.560
a support service for people that
are feeling despairing or suicidal.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.960
The service was set up in the 50s
by a vicar,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.160
and he started it because he had to
conduct the funeral of a young girl

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.480
who had taken her own life.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.040
The reason she took her own life
is that she'd started her periods

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.000
and thought she'd contracted some
terrible disease and was dying.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.280
She was deeply ashamed
of what was happening to her

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.440
and had no one to talk to about it.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.920
It was this that inspired
the vicar, Chad Varah,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.320
to set up the Samaritans
as he didn't want people to feel

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.120
that there were things
that they couldn't talk about,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.960
particularly things
that they were ashamed of.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.120
He believed that you should
be able to sit with someone,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.320
no matter what they're going through,
without feeling the need

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.240
to answer back or offer solutions
or problem solve.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.160
And a really central way of doing
that is through using silence

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.080
and giving them space
to explain what's going on for them.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:32.480
And frankly,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.520
if normal conversations could involve
a little bit more silence like that

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.440
I think the world would be
a much happier place.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.040
So silence is also at the heart
of most major world religions.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.120
In Buddhism silence
is really highly valued

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.400
and conversation can be seen

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.960
as something that gets in the way
of our experiences.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.920
And if you strip away
all of that language

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.880
you're left with a deeper
understanding of your own experiences

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.160
in the world and your connection
to other people.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.080
But can too much silence
be a bad thing?

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.560
There's a condition known as Assidy
which was experienced by monks

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.600
who spent prolonged periods
in silence

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.760
and it's basically distinguished
by a feeling of torpor,

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.320
of not being able to do anything

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.760
and that's specifically associated
with spending long periods of time

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.120
without speaking to anybody.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.120
I also spoke to people
who had spent more time

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.280
than probably most of us
would want to in silence.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:16.520
One of them was a Buddhist

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.480
who spent nine months
on a silent and solitary retreat

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.040
and the silence had
a really profound effect on him.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.560
After a certain amount of time

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.120
he felt like he was losing control
of both his mind and his body.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.200
He actually felt
like he was having a heart attack.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.880
He knew logically he'd just
pulled a muscle in his chest

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.840
but without anyone to say, "I've
pulled a muscle in my chest" to,

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.600
the pain that he was feeling,
the sensations of panic

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.960
meant that he actually really thought
that he was dying

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.240
and the only way that
he could snap out of that

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.640
was by calling a friend of his.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.040
And it was only once he
had words and language

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.800
to attach to what was
happening to him

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.000
that he was able to make sense of it
and eventually feel better.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.040
So that Buddhist actually described
silence to me as being like love -

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.320
it's something that, instinctively,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.800
we all feel we want
and need in our lives

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.800
but silence, like love,
is really strong medicine

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.120
and it can actually
completely tear you apart.

